{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LANDED as change 1063 (patchset 2).\n\nPatchset 1 earned Verified-1. Root cause: `conftest` is NOT a stable module name. Several conftests in this repo share it, and under xdist `sys.modules['conftest']` ends up bound to whichever was imported most recently. The module-level imports in test_rebar_repo_isolation.py resolved at collection time, but a function-local `from conftest import _clone_template` ran later and picked up the ROOT conftest -> ImportError.\n\nIt passed `pytest tests/interfaces` and failed the full default suite, because the tier's own conftest wins only when it is the sole one loaded. My verification ran the tier alone, which is exactly the scope where this bug is invisible.\n\nFix: extracted the shared helpers into tests/interfaces/_store_template.py, a uniquely-named module, so nothing depends on the ambiguous `conftest` name. Re-verified against the FULL default suite: 7961 passed, 0 failures; make lint + make typecheck clean.\n\nMeasured result: tests/interfaces at -n 4 went 270.7s -> 186.5s (31%) while running 9 MORE tests (1397 passed).\n\nDiscovery worth recording beyond the plan: bare `git worktree repair` is not merely the silent no-op every review round described. Run inside a copy it reports 'repair: .git file incorrect' against the SOURCE and rewrites the SOURCE's .tickets-tracker/.git to point into the copy — it corrupts the store it is run against. An early version of the sentinel test ran it against a copy of the live session template and sacrificed it, which is why _clone_template now asserts the SOURCE is intact before every copy."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785453061512089001,"uuid":"8dc847e0-594e-4a8f-b75a-296a039268f6"}